1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This Debian desktop is running inside a VirtualBox guest created through the VAMP release.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
In the installed extension, find the VM by name and select Start or View Screen.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Look for both the connection indicator and the guest desktop. Now let’s repeat the steps.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,467
Download the VSIX from the release and compare its checksum. In VS Code running as administrator, install from VSIX and reload the window. The Windows host also needs VirtualBox 7.

5
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:50,600
Send this request to Claude Code or Codex running on your PC. Ask it to list the local virtual machines after it connects.

6
00:00:50,600 --> 00:01:02,933
Start with an installed Debian golden image. Ask your AI to list completed images. On a fresh setup, choose a Debian installer ISO in VM Manager and finish Create and Start first.

7
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:13,867
Ask the AI to create a VM from your chosen image under a new name. The example name is on screen. Review the source and destination in the import preview before approving.

8
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:24,133
An import returns the new VM identifier and leaves the VM powered off. Ask the AI to start that VM in GUI mode. The native guest window opens.

9
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:35,800
In VS Code, open VM Manager and select VirtualBox. Find the name you just created, choose Start or View Screen, and check the connection and the Debian desktop inside the editor.

10
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:45,800
On Linux, the same release received a Debian DVD and a new VM name. This is the real guest screen while the VAMP-launched Packer job installs the base system.

11
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:58,333
The build guest reached the Debian desktop. The separately deployed VM showed its boot screen over local VNC, then paused with a KVM hardware error on this nested host. Login was unverified.

12
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:08,333
For Hyper-V, prepare an internal switch and a reserved static address. The released VM creation tool installs Debian and uses Vagrant to deploy a separate VM.

13
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:21,000
Windows could not allocate three gigabytes on the first attempt. With two gigabytes and the other guests stopped, verification passed after reboot. VMConnect showed the Debian welcome screen on the deployed VM.

14
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:31,267
Check the host, VM name and visible guest screen before continuing. The repository walkthrough includes copyable requests and the verification conditions for this lab.
